(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatological-infestations-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatological Infestations Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 33-year-old woman presents with a rash that shows discrete circumscribed papules and, in the older areas, crusting and scaling. Which conclusion follows from the crusting?","choices":["The subcutaneous tissue is involved","The epidermis has been affected","The infection is bacterial in origin","The lesions are resolving spontaneously"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — crust and scale indicate epidermal involvement, which narrows the reaction pattern. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl has an intensely painful sting on the arm with two parallel rows of purpura at the site after handling a caterpillar. Which caterpillar is responsible?","choices":["The tarantula, whose shed hairs embed in skin and in the eyes","The asp or puss caterpillar, described as the most poisonous","The gypsy moth caterpillar, which causes caterpillar dermatitis","The processionary caterpillar, which causes systemic reactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the train-track pattern of purpura is characteristic. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy brushed against a caterpillar in the garden and now has multiple itchy erythematous papules arranged in linear streaks on his forearm. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["An asp or puss caterpillar sting, the most poisonous of the group","A processionary caterpillar reaction with urticaria and angio-oedema","Cutaneous larva migrans acquired from soil in the same garden","Erucism, or caterpillar dermatitis, from a gypsy moth caterpillar"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a pruritic dermatitis with papules in linear streaks. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man bitten by a brown recluse two days ago has a darkening area at the site plus nausea, headache and chills. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Systemic symptoms mean the wound requires wide surgical excision within the next day","Systemic symptoms develop one to two days after the bite and necrosis at two to three days","Systemic symptoms indicate secondary bacterial infection and need immediate antibiotics","Systemic symptoms this early exclude a brown recluse and suggest a black widow bite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — eschar forms between days five and seven, then deep ulcers. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman in Missouri was bitten while cleaning an attic. The site now shows a dark purple centre surrounded by a pale blanched ring, and beyond that a large irregular area of redness. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A black widow spider bite","A hobo spider bite","Early cellulitis at the site","A brown recluse spider bite"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the red, white and blue sign is the hallmark of this envenomation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 4-month-old infant has irritability, poor sleep and crusted indurated nodules on the trunk and in the skin folds, with excoriations extending onto the scalp and neck. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis of infancy","Seborrhoeic dermatitis, or cradle cap","Bullous impetigo of the trunk and folds","Scabies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — infants, the elderly and the immunocompromised can have head and neck involvement, and infants get these nodules. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman finds an attached tick on her leg after gardening in Maine. Which is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Start a ten to fourteen day course of doxycycline the same day as the bite","Send serology the same day and treat only if the result comes back positive","Remove the tick immediately, then watch for the rash and constitutional symptoms","Leave the tick in place until it detaches, since removal can force in more organism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antibiotics are indicated once the disease is diagnosed, at all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has caterpillar dermatitis with visible fine hairs still on the skin. Which physical measure is most appropriate?","choices":["Strip the hairs off with adhesive tape","Scrape the area with a card edge held flat","Comb the area after applying hair moisturiser","Apply blue ink and look for a zigzag line"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside antihistamines, menthol or camphor, and topical corticosteroid. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl has been treated for head lice and her mother asks what to do about the house. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Wash everything at 60 degrees Celsius and treat every household member immediately","Engage a professional exterminator to treat cracks and crevices around the beds","Put clothing and bedding from the past week in the dryer or bag it for two weeks, wash combs and vacuum","Have the home professionally fumigated and then discard all the pillows and the soft furnishings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — fumigation is specifically not recommended. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man asks how bedbugs got into his flat when he keeps it clean. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["They spread in the clothing and baggage of travellers and visitors, and in second-hand mattresses and laundry","They spread by direct skin-to-skin contact lasting fifteen to twenty minutes with an infested person","They spread through contaminated clothing that cannot be washed or changed regularly","They spread from soil or sand contaminated with the faeces of dogs and cats"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the infestation is not a marker of poor hygiene. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has had head lice treated twice without success. Which is the most appropriate explanation to give the parents?","choices":["Occlusive home remedies such as mayonnaise or mineral oil are the recommended next step","Nits remaining in the hair after treatment prove the infestation is still active","Resistance is increasing, so a multimodal approach combining physical and chemical methods is warranted","Failure means the diagnosis is wrong, so the itching is more likely to be seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pediculicidal effect is read twenty-four hours after application. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has a mild local reaction to a bee sting with pain and a small area of redness. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intramuscular epinephrine with transfer to an emergency department","A systemic corticosteroid course tapered over the following week","An epinephrine auto-injector prescription and desensitisation referral","Cleaning, ice, and possibly injection of local anaesthetic for pain control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — management is graded to the severity of the reaction. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman stung by a wasp develops generalised urticaria, lip swelling and wheeze within ten minutes. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Epinephrine, given intramuscularly or subcutaneously, with emergency transfer and supportive measures","An oral antihistamine with a short course of prednisolone and review the following day","Ice to the sting site with injection of a local anaesthetic for control of the pain","Calcium gluconate with a muscle relaxant and a benzodiazepine given intravenously"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — this is an anaphylactic reaction rather than a severe local one. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has early Lyme disease with erythema migrans. Which is the most appropriate oral antibiotic?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Amoxicillin","Doxycycline","Azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the alternative first line for early disease in children and pregnant patients. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman who hikes in an endemic area asks how to protect herself. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Take prophylactic doxycycline through the summer months while she continues to hike","Rely on showering promptly after each hike, since this removes ticks before they attach","Avoid tick habitat, use DEET, PMD or picaridin reapplied about every two hours, and treat clothing with pyrethrins","Request the human Lyme vaccine, which is recommended for anyone living in or visiting an endemic region"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is no human vaccine, though one exists for dogs. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever is on day 4 of illness. The indirect immunofluorescence assay has been sent. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Wait for the assay result, since the gold standard test should guide the choice of antibiotic","Start amoxicillin now, since doxycycline should be reserved for confirmed disease","Repeat the assay in three days and treat only if it has become positive by then","Start doxycycline now, since treatment should begin by day 5 and the assay is rarely diagnostic before day 7"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treatment must not wait for the serology to return. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"The parent of an 8-year-old treated for head lice asks when she can return to school. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["She can return now — a no-nit policy is not recommended because of the school absence it causes","She must stay home until every single nit has been combed out of her hair completely","She must stay home for two full weeks after the treatment has been completed","She must stay home until a repeat wet comb finds no nits and no live lice"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that position comes from the American Academy of Pediatrics. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man treated for hookworm folliculitis has persistent follicular pustules after one course of albendazole. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Excise the affected follicles, since surgery clears resistant lesions","Apply cryotherapy to each pustule, since freezing kills the larvae","Repeat the course, since hookworm folliculitis may need repeated treatments","Switch to topical therapy, which is more effective for the follicular form"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the folliculitic form is specifically noted to be more resistant. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman treated for scabies three weeks ago still has itchy papules, though no new burrows are seen and her household has been treated. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral antibiotics directed at staphylococcal superinfection of the excoriations","A mid to high potency topical corticosteroid, or intralesional triamcinolone acetonide","A second full course of permethrin applied overnight to the whole skin surface","Oral ivermectin every two weeks for two to three doses alongside topical therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — persistent post-scabietic papules are a recognised complication. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl with cercarial dermatitis has pain and swelling with the itch, worst about two days after exposure. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral doxycycline for ten days to cover the organism carried in the fresh water","Antihistamines, oatmeal baths, antipruritic lotions, aspirin for pain and topical or oral glucocorticoids","Albendazole 400 mg by mouth daily for three days to clear the parasite from the skin","Overnight topical permethrin applied to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the pain and swelling peak at 48 to 72 hours before settling. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman removed a tick after hiking in South Carolina and asks whether she should take an antibiotic to prevent Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["A five-day course of doxycycline should be started because the area is endemic","Serology should be sent now and prophylaxis started if it returns positive","Prophylactic antibiotic therapy is not recommended; prevention rests on avoidance, clothing, tick checks and DEET","A single dose of doxycycline should be taken now to prevent the illness from developing at all over the coming days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — she should return if fever or headache develop. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 30-year-old pregnant woman has been in contact with a household member diagnosed with scabies but has no symptoms and no lesions. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Withhold treatment, since pregnant patients are treated only where scabies is documented","Treat her with permethrin now, since all household contacts are treated regardless","Treat her with oral ivermectin now, since topical agents are avoided in pregnancy","Treat her only after delivery, whatever her symptoms or examination findings show"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — treating all infested persons in the group applies to those with documented infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man treated for scabies has scrubbed his skin with a strong antibacterial soap several times a day and now has widespread irritation. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Excessive washing with harsh soap can worsen the skin irritation and should be stopped","Vigorous washing is essential to remove the mites from the surface of the skin","The irritation confirms the treatment has failed and a second agent is needed","The irritation indicates a secondary bacterial infection needing oral antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the permethrin course itself is what treats the infestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with suspected body lice has no visible organisms on skin examination. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Examine the seams of his clothing for nits and shake the clothing over white paper","Wet comb the scalp with water and conditioner using a fine-toothed nit comb","Pluck a hair from the affected area and examine it under the microscope for nits","Scrape an unexcoriated papule with mineral oil and examine the smear microscopically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lice will move onto the paper where they can be seen. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with coronary artery disease has a confirmed black widow bite with severe abdominal pain. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Observe him for six hours, since all the symptoms of this envenomation resolve within that time","Hospitalise him, since the very old, the very young and those with cardiovascular disease are at increased risk","Discharge him with oral analgesia, since death from this bite is uncommon in any age group","Refer him urgently for surgical debridement before necrosis develops at the bite site"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treat with calcium gluconate, narcotic analgesia, muscle relaxants and benzodiazepines. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man who keeps a pet tarantula has an itchy papular rash on his forearms and a red, gritty eye. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Topical corticosteroid for the skin, with ophthalmology referral for the eye","Oral antihistamine for the skin, with artificial tears and review in two weeks","Oral ivermectin for the skin, with topical antibiotic drops for the affected eye","Surgical removal of each embedded hair from the skin and from the eye surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — shed hairs embed in skin and eyes, causing conjunctivitis and corneal granuloma. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has suspected Rocky Mountain spotted fever. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous ceftriaxone at a weight-based dose for a course of ten days","Doxycycline at 2.2 mg per kilogram by mouth twice daily for five to ten days","Amoxicillin at a weight-based dose by mouth for a course of fourteen days","Azithromycin at a weight-based dose by mouth for a course of five days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is used in children and pregnancy here, with desensitisation where contraindicated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man is travelling to Nigeria and asks how to avoid tungiasis. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Avoid contact with the bedding and underclothing of anyone who is infested","Inspect hotel headboards and picture frames before unpacking any luggage","Do not walk barefoot or in sandals on beaches, and do not sit directly on the sand","Use DEET-based repellent reapplied every two hours and perform daily body checks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — Nigeria, the Caribbean, India and Brazil are the named endemic settings. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with a hobo spider bite asks what to expect. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The wound will need surgical reconstruction and healing will take several months","The symptoms will resolve within six hours with intravenous calcium gluconate","The lesion will continue to advance daily until antiparasitic treatment is given","The wound heals within several weeks and headaches may last a week; treatment is supportive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — death from severe systemic effects, including aplastic anaemia, is rare. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with bedbug bites has scratched several open and one is now crusted and tender. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["An oral antibiotic covering methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus for ten days","Overnight topical permethrin to the whole skin surface with a repeat dose at one week","Surgical excision of the affected lesions followed by tetanus prophylaxis","Topical antiseptic lotion or antibiotic cream for the secondary infection, with local wound care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — topical steroids or oral antihistamines address the pruritus separately. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
